  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nMultiple rounds of rain showers and storms are expected to\ncontinue slowly across the area through the rest of the day\ntoday. Going into the evening, a strong LLJ develops and will\nhelp advect more moisture from the Atlantic into the area, and \nin combination with a shortwave system moving into the \nPoconos/Catskills area this evening, additional heavy rainfall \nwith rates of 1-2 inches per hour is possible.\n\nThis system will slowly pivot eastward, which becomes the main\ncause for the potential for flash flooding. PWATs aren't too\nhigh (around 1.0 to 1.5), but due to the slow progression and\npivot of this system, there remains a risk for flash flooding \nmainly for areas east of I-81. \n\nForecasted rainfall totals will range from 1 to 3 inches across\nthe region. As mentioned, the strong LLJ and terrain effect \nwill likely lead to an area of higher rainfall amounts, where \nlocally totals will push into the 3 to 5 inch range in the \nCatskills and Poconos.The Flood Watch continues for affected \nareas until tomorrow evening.\n\nSome instability is available for some storm development, but\nchances for a storm to become severe is relatively low. With any\nstorm, moderate to heavy rainfall is possible.\n\nAs this system begins to move east and slightly northward\novernight, wrap-around moisture will continue into tomorrow,\nmainly for Central NY, highlighted over the Finger Lakes region.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThis system will drift up the coast into New England by Thursday, \nthrough wraparound moisture on the back side of the system will \ncontinue to support light showers over the region through the\nend of the work week. A ridge will begin to push in from the \nwest with drier air beginning Friday afternoon, with rain\nshowers coming to an end by the late afternoon/early evening\nhours.\n\nOver the weekend, a trough takes shape over the Northeast once \nagain as a low pressure system develops in the Southwest. The \nflow around this low will tap into the Gulf and Atlantic, \npotentially advecting moisture northward into the region. There \nis uncertainty though if surface high pressure keeps conditions \ndry through Sunday or if showers develop with the trough digging\nin. Chances for showers may be better early next week as a cold\nfront drops in from the north.",
